@doc Markdown.doc"""
   FurutaPredictionModel(; name, idparams, g)

The plant model the MPC predicts with: the `QubePendulum` with one signal added, the
pendulum's energy.

This is the very model the controller is closed around -- there is no second, hand-written
set of equations to keep in step with the plant. `furuta_mpc_dynamics` (src/mpc.jl) compiles
it with `MultibodyComponents.multibody`, taking `qube.voltage` as the input, and hands the
result to `MPCComponents.continuous_dynamics`; what is left in Julia there is that
compilation and nothing else, since no Dyad component can express it.

`pendulum_energy_ratio` is the pendulum's mechanical energy relative to hanging -- the
kinetic energy of its rotation about the elbow (its inertia about the centre of mass `Jp`
plus the parallel-axis term `mp*l^2`) plus the height of its centre of mass -- divided by
the value at rest upright. So it is 0 hanging at rest and 1 upright at rest, and `FurutaMPC`
weights it as a fifth controlled output with the reference 1, which is what makes the
controller swing up. The masses and lengths are read off `qube` rather than restated here,
so the energy cannot drift from the plant it describes when `idparams` changes.

It is the same quantity the energy swing-up (`Energy`) pumps, but not the same expression:
`Energy` omits the parallel-axis term, so the two disagree by nearly a factor of four on the
kinetic part. Which of them is meant is a physics question deliberately left open --
`EnergySwingup`'s gain was tuned against its own expression.

The MPC's four states are `qube.shoulder_joint.phi`, `qube.elbow_joint.phi` and their
derivatives (`FURUTA_MPC_STATES`, named so the state input does not depend on the compiler's
state order). The plant's `shoulder_angle`/`elbow_angle` outputs are those joint angles
exactly, so the hardware measurements and the estimated velocities map onto the model states
one to one.

## Parameters:

| Name         | Description                         | Units  |   Default value |
| ------------ | ----------------------------------- | ------ | --------------- |
| `idparams`         | Physical parameter set of the pendulum being predicted (see dyad/definitions.jl)                         | --  |   identified |
| `g`         | Gravitational acceleration, as `world` applies it                         | --  |   9.81 |

